#!/usr/bin/python3
"""et-usaged: log every process that holds an ET-SoC-1 card on this host.

Part of tools/lab/et-usage in the et-soc1-prototyping repository (see README.md there).

How: inotify watches on the card nodes (/dev/et<N>_mgmt, /dev/et<N>_ops) and the card lock files
(/run/lock/etsoc-shire<N>.lock, only for a card N that has a node) for open and close; on an event (after a 20 ms
debounce; at most 10 scans a second, at most 10% of the time, and once a second for lock-only events) and every
15 s, a scan of the /proc/<pid>/fd links finds who holds them. A lock-file fd counts only while it holds the lock
(the "lock:" line of its fdinfo), so a process waiting in flock holds nothing. One JSON line per (process, card) hold
goes to /var/log/et-usage/<local date>.jsonl when the hold ends, with heartbeats, the change of the driver's message
counters, the current holders in /run/et-usage/now.json, and the open holds in /var/log/et-usage/open.json (written
as "lost_end" holds by the next start if this process dies without SIGTERM).

Never: open a /dev/et* node (inotify_add_watch and lstat do not open it), read a process's argv, environment or cwd
(only /proc/<pid>/stat, the Uid line of status, comm, the fd links, the fdinfo of a lock-file fd, and maps: at start
and after an open no scan explained, every process's maps are searched for the node paths and only a match is kept),
read a card attribute other than devnum and {mgmt,ops}_vq_stats/msg_count (none at all of a card given to
--skip-pci), or write to sysfs.

Python 3 standard library only. Runs under systemd (et-usaged.service) as the et-usage user with CAP_SYS_PTRACE and
CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH (to read other users' fd links), or as any user for tests (it then sees only its own processes).
"""

import argparse
import ctypes
import fcntl
import fnmatch
import glob
import json
import os
import pwd
import re
import select
import shlex
import signal
import socket
import stat
